   "evidence": "NormalForm.lean exposes `pair_def` (108), `pair_congr_ae_left` (144), `pair_congr_ae_right` (155), `pair_pow` (186), `pair_smul` (216) and nothing else about `pair`; PlusFunctional.lean develops both slots: `plusFunctional_const_mul_right` (204), `plusOneMinus_const_mul` (212), `plusFunctional_add_right` (228), `plusOneMinus_add` (252).",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/QFT/Factorization/Evolution/Kernel/NormalForm.lean",
   "fix": "Add `pair_const_mul_right (P) (c) (\u03c6) : P.pair (fun z => c * \u03c6 z) = c * P.pair \u03c6` (unconditional, as `plusFunctional_const_mul_right` is, since `intervalIntegral.integral_const_mul` and `plusOneMinus_const_mul` need no integrability) tagged `@[simp]`, and `pair_add_right` under the two integrability hypotheses that `plusFunctional_add_right`/`plusOneMinus_add` carry.",
   "issue": "The pairing's characteristic property \u2014 linearity in the test function \u2014 is missing from the public API. `KernelForm.pair` has `pair_smul`/`moment_smul` in the kernel slot and `pair_congr_ae_left`/`pair_congr_ae_right` in both slots, but no `pair_const_mul_right` or `pair_add_right`, so a consumer cannot conclude `P.pair (fun z => c * \u03c6 z) = c * P.pair \u03c6` or additivity without going through `pair_def` and redoing the integral bookkeeping. The next step the PR names for this roadmap line (the du Bois-Reymond uniqueness of the normal form, \u00a70.3/acceptance example 5) is a statement about `pair` as a linear functional.",
   "line": "216"
  }
 ],
